  "plans": [
    {
      "hypothesis": "The rejected drawing measured the wrong phase. Switching flake_phase from dark to bright should make the coloured flakes coincide with the long bright primary-cementite plates on the next review. This also needs three more changes: raising min_area_px about 10x and min_aspect_for_orientation to 3, so ledeburite dots are dropped; turning off the grain-boundary-alpha filter, so long plates are not greyed out; and turning off scratch detection, so plates are not inpainted as scratches. The claim is falsified if the reviewer again sees the plates left unmeasured, or sees them fused with the bright eutectic network into large branched blobs.",
      "rationale": "The only evidence is the reviewer's reading of the real drawing. It judged the phase family correct but the phase wrong, with about 90% confidence: the dark pearlite and ledeburite islands were measured, and the bright cementite plates were left white. The twin is untrusted (contrast ratio 0.42, minority fraction ratio 0.58), and its scores (precision 0.03, worst family 3.5x tolerance) support nothing, so no twin search runs and these start params go straight to review. I change only params tied to the reviewer's evidence:\n- flake_phase: dark -> bright, as the reviewer directed.\n- min_area_px: 19 -> 200, about 10x as asked, to drop the fine bright cementite inside the ledeburite.\n- min_aspect_for_orientation: 1.5 -> 3.0. The reviewer asked for >= 4, but 3 is the maximum this param accepts.\n- gb_alpha_length_factor: 4 -> 0 (off). Primary plates are by nature much longer than the median flake and cross their neighbours at large angles, so the default rule would grey them out as grain-boundary alpha.\n- detect_scratches: off. The one detected 'scratch' looked to the reviewer like a plate edge, and long straight bright plates are exactly what the scratch detector would inpaint away.\n- sigma: 0.8 -> 1.5, to smooth the fine bright eutectic texture.\n- phase_offset: 0.05, to thin the bright mask slightly so plates detach from the eutectic network. This is the closest available stand-in for the requested morphological opening.\n\nLimitations stated plainly:\n- The method has no opening and no junction-splitting param. Crossing plates will form X and star shapes. The skeleton branch_test will leave such shapes out of the statistics rather than split them, so orientation coverage of the plates may stay poor. No current parameter fixes this; a line or structure-tensor fit on the bright mask would be needed.\n- Bright is the majority phase here (about 0.55), so the eutectic cementite network may still merge with the plates.\n- The nearest earlier set (needles_L23) failed under the same reading.\n\nConfidence: about 60% that the coloured objects will at least be cementite after this change, and about 25% that the drawings will be accepted. No number from this run should be claimed until the twin is rebuilt to look like long bright crossing plates and the rotation check beats 0.3.",
      "reading": "phase",
      "flake_phase": "bright",
      "start_params_json": "{\"mode\": \"phase\", \"flake_phase\": \"bright\", \"min_area_px\": 200, \"min_aspect_for_orientation\": 3.0, \"gb_alpha_length_factor\": 0, \"detect_scratches\": false, \"sigma\": 1.5, \"phase_offset\": 0.05}",
      "search_space_json": "{\"phase_offset\": [0.0, 0.03, 0.05, 0.08, 0.12], \"min_area_px\": [100, 200, 300, 500], \"sigma\": [1.0, 1.5, 2.0, 3.0], \"h_px\": [0.0, 0.4, 1.0, 2.0], \"min_aspect_for_orientation\": [2.0, 2.5, 3.0], \"merge_saddle_ratio\": [0.3, 0.5, 0.7, 1.0]}"
