      "verdict": "reject",
      "reading_correct": "no",
      "reason": "Reading: the phase reading is the right family, but the wrong phase was chosen. The micrograph is labelled primary cementite (white cast iron). What a metallographer measures are the long, straight, bright primary-cementite plates. They run up to roughly 1 mm, are tens of \u00b5m thick, and cross each other at many angles. In the drawing those plates are left white and unmeasured. The coloured 'flakes' are something else: dark pearlite and ledeburite islands and dots in the eutectic background, plus dark etch and pull-out patches. Some of those patches are shown in grey, and some are wrongly coloured as large irregular purple, orange and olive blobs. So all 10,015 'flakes' and the summary numbers describe the wrong objects. S = 0.035, AR 2.06 and coverage 0.345 are numbers for the eutectic texture, not for the plates. An S near 0 might even be roughly true for the plates, but it was not measured on them, so it cannot be claimed. Failures seen: (a) Matrix and eutectic islands are taken as flakes, everywhere. (b) The real flakes are missed completely. (c) Large dark etch stains are counted as flakes. (d) Scratches: one short line near the centre is marked as a scratch. I cannot confirm it is a real scratch; it looks like part of a plate edge. Twin: it is untrusted and does not resemble this image (short dark dashes in grey colonies versus long bright crossing plates). Its own scores are also poor: flake precision 0.03, crossing-scratch recall 0.05, aspect-ratio bias -41%, worst family 3.5\u00d7 tolerance. It gives no support to any number. The closest earlier set (needles_L23, distance 0.0, same reading) failed: holdout 4.2\u00d7, rotation check 0.70. Confidence that the reading is wrong: high, about 90%. Confidence that no number in this run is usable: high.",
      "next_step": "Change the reading to the other phase: flake_phase: dark -> bright. Also raise the minimum flake size so the fine bright cementite inside the ledeburite is not taken. Set min_flake_area to about 10\u00d7 its current value, and require a minimum flake length of about 100 \u00b5m and aspect ratio \u2265 4 for 'used' flakes. Add a light morphological opening (radius about 3\u20135 px) before labelling, to detach the plates from the bright eutectic network. Crossing plates will merge into X and star shapes. To handle them, enable splitting of branched objects at junctions (skeleton split, or a line or Hough-based plate fit) instead of greying them out. Otherwise the branched filter will discard most of the plates. Rebuild the twin to match this image: long bright plates 5\u201340 px thick, random crossings, a dark mottled eutectic background with dark stains. Only then re-tune on the twin, and require the rotation check to beat 0.3 before accepting. If the method's labelling cannot split crossing plates, say that no current parameter fixes this, and measure plate orientation with a line or structure-tensor approach on the bright mask instead."
    },
    {
      "verdict": "reject",
      "reading_correct": "yes",
      "reason": "Phase reading is correct for this image: the long bright primary-cementite needles in the ledeburite matrix are the flakes. The coloured regions in the drawing do lie on those bright needles. That contradicts the stated 'flakes are the dark phase', so the polarity setting needs checking.\n\nThe drawings do not support the numbers:\n(1) Long needles are cut along their length into several pieces, and the pieces sometimes get different colours. This happens mostly where needles cross each other, e.g. top-right orange/purple needles and the centre yellow/magenta pair.\n(2) Many clearly visible needles stay grey, so they are not used. Coverage is only 0.19, and several near-horizontal needles in the left half are missing.\n(3) Many small coloured specks in the eutectic/ledeburite regions are matrix lamellae taken as flakes. They make up much of n=359 and pull the median aspect ratio down to 2.1, whereas the visible needles have aspect ratios of roughly 15\u201350. The aspect-ratio figure is wrong by about an order of magnitude.\nS=0.21 (near-random needle network) is plausible, but its value is uncertain given the contamination. No scratches are visible, and none were reported. Colony/grain boundaries are not the issue here; crossings between needles are.\n\nThe twin is useless as evidence. Its short-dash colonies do not resemble crossing needles. Flake recall is 0, its drawing shows dashes merged into large grey blobs, and it is untrusted.\n\nThe closest prior set, needles_L23 (distance 0, same reading), also failed: rotation check 0.70, holdout worst family 4.2. Repeating that configuration will not succeed.\n\nConfidence: high that this should be rejected; moderate on the exact causes.",
      "next_step": "Parameter names below are illustrative; map them to the method's actual names.\n(a) Set flake polarity: dark -> bright, so the stated reading matches what is segmented.\n(b) Raise min_flake_area by about 5\u201310x, to roughly the area of a needle 3 widths long, and require minimum aspect ratio for use 1.5 -> 4. This drops the ledeburite specks.\n(c) Split at crossings by orientation rather than merging or fragmenting there: enable skeleton/branch splitting with an angle threshold of about 15\u00b0, and re-join collinear fragments across gaps of up to 2 needle widths (merge_collinear: off -> on, max_angle 10\u00b0).\n(d) Regenerate the twin as long, straight, randomly oriented crossing needles (width 5\u201320 px, length 200\u20131500 px) on a fine eutectic-textured matrix, and do not score until it is trusted.\nIf (b) and (c) cannot both keep crossing needles separate and remove matrix fragments, state that the current method cannot measure needle aspect ratios on this set."
    }
